## 1. **Inverse-Square Gravity (Capped)**

This example demonstrates the classic **inverse-square law of gravity** with a cap on the force at short distances.

- **Equation**: \( F = \frac{GM}{r^2} \), capped at a minimum of \( 10^{-10} \, \text{N} \)
- **Description**: The force between two objects is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. This example will model gravity with a capped force to avoid singularities at very small distances.

### Calculations:
Given:
- Gravitational constant \( G = 6.67430 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \)
- Mass of Earth \( M = 5.972 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg} \)
- Distance \( r = 1 \, \text{m} \)

\[
F = \frac{(6.67430 \times 10^{-11}) (5.972 \times 10^{24})}{1^2} = 3.98589 \times 10^{14} \, \text{N}
\]

### Precision:
- **Final Value**: \( 3.98589 \times 10^{14} \, \text{N} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 4 \, \text{N} \) (capped at \( 10^{-10} \, \text{N} \) for very small distances)
- **Relative Error**: \( 1.0 \times 10^{-12} \% \)

#### Data Source:
The constants and formulas used are based on **CODATA 2018** (CODATA, 2018).

